<date date-type="accepted"><day>19</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></date>
</history><permissions><!--&#x000a9; 2026 European Society of Cardiology and Wiley Periodicals, Inc.--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s). <italic toggle="yes">Annals of Noninvasive Electrocardiology</italic> published by Wiley Periodicals LLC.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use and distribution in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited, the use is non&#x02010;commercial and no modifications or adaptations are made.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:ANEC-31-e70135.pdf"/><abstract><title>ABSTRACT</title><p>Asynchronous pacing itself does not directly lead to the development of malignant ventricular arrhythmias. However, acute myocardial ischemia caused by acute reduction in coronary blood flow can result in a &#x0201c;vulnerable myocardium&#x0201d; and simultaneously impair pacemaker sensing function. Such a scenario may give rise to unintended asynchronous pacing, which can act as a trigger for malignant ventricular arrhythmias. We present a case illustrating how ischemia&#x02010;induced pacemaker sensing failure contributed to life&#x02010;threatening ventricular arrhythmias, highlighting the critical interplay between myocardial perfusion status and pacemaker behavior.</p></abstract><abstract abstract-type="graphical"><p>We present a case involving pacemaker sensing dysfunction caused by acute reduction in blood flow in the right coronary artery. The pacing spikes occurred progressively earlier on the T waves, eventually resulting in R&#x02010;on&#x02010;T pacing that triggered VF.<boxed-text position="anchor" content-type="graphic" id="anec70135-blkfxd-0001"><graphic xlink:href="ANEC-31-e70135-g002.jpg" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1"/></boxed-text>
</p></abstract><kwd-group kwd-group-type="author-generated"><kwd id="anec70135-kwd-0001">asynchronous pacing</kwd><kwd id="anec70135-kwd-0002">no&#x02010;reflow phenomenon</kwd><kwd id="anec70135-kwd-0003">pacemaker&#x02010;induced ventricular fibrillation</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group id="funding-0001"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>Provincial Natural Science Foundation of Hunan</institution></institution-wrap>
</funding-source><award-id>2022JJ40672</award-id></award-group></funding-group><counts><fig-count count="2"/><table-count count="0"/><page-count count="3"/><word-count count="1500"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>January 2026</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.6.6 mode:remove_FC converted:09.12.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta><notes><p content-type="self-citation">
<mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="anec70135-cit-1001">
<string-name>
<surname>Wu</surname>, <given-names>B.</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<given-names>Y.</given-names>
<surname>Chen</surname>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<given-names>J.</given-names>
<surname>Tang</surname>
</string-name>, and <string-name>
<given-names>J.</given-names>
<surname>He</surname>
</string-name>. <year>2026</year>. &#x0201c;<article-title>Acute Reduction in Blood Flow in the Right Coronary Artery After <styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">PCI</styled-content> Facilitates Pacemaker&#x02010;Induced Ventricular Fibrillation</article-title>.&#x0201d; <source>Annals of Noninvasive Electrocardiology</source>
<volume>31</volume>, no. <issue>1</issue>: <elocation-id>e70135</elocation-id>. <pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/anec.70135</pub-id>.</mixed-citation>
</p></notes></front><body id="anec70135-body-0001"><sec id="anec70135-sec-0001"><label>1</label><title>Introduction</title><p>Temporary pacemakers are widely used for bradyrhythmia of various etiologies and are generally considered safe. However, under certain pathological conditions, the sensing dysfunction of a temporary pacemaker may act as a &#x0201c;double&#x02010;edged sword,&#x0201d; potentially triggering malignant ventricular arrhythmias.</p></sec><sec id="anec70135-sec-0002"><label>2</label><title>Case Presentation</title><p>A 70&#x02010;year&#x02010;old male presented with a 3&#x02010;year history of exertional angina, which had worsened the day prior to admission. His medical history was notable for pulmonary tuberculosis and poorly controlled hypertension. Initial laboratory tests revealed elevated levels of creatine kinase (CK) at 465.0&#x02009;U/L (reference range 50.0&#x02013;310.0&#x02009;U/L), CK&#x02010;MB at 49.4&#x02009;U/L (reference range 0&#x02013;24.0&#x02009;U/L), and troponin I at 8.01&#x02009;ng/mL (reference range &#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05&#x02009;ng/mL). The patient's admission electrocardiogram (ECG) showed a pathological Q wave and inverted T wave in lead III and biphasic and flattened T waves in the anterior precordial leads. A diagnosis of non&#x02013;ST&#x02010;elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI) was done. The patient was clinically stable after his presentation with NSTEMI. He was referred for cardiac catheterization later that day. Coronary angiography (CAG) revealed multivessel stenosis; two stents were placed in the left anterior descending artery. His symptoms improved following the percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) and he was admitted to the cardiac care unit (CCU). The ECG after the PCI demonstrated T reversion in the anterior precordial leads compared with the admission ECG, and troponin levels gradually decreased. Considering that the CAG revealed diffuse 80% stenosis in the proximal to mid&#x02010;segment of the right coronary artery and the ECG showed a Q wave and T wave inversion in lead III, it was reasonable to treat the right coronary artery during the same hospitalization. Four days later, the patient underwent a second PCI. A no&#x02010;reflow phenomenon occurred during stent deployment in the right coronary artery, resulting in bradycardia with a heart rate of 35&#x02009;bpm and hypotension with a blood pressure of 60/48&#x02009;mmHg. A temporary pacemaker and an intra&#x02010;aortic balloon pump (IABP) were inserted (Video&#x000a0;<xref rid="anec70135-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S1</xref>). The tip of the pacing catheter was located in the apex of the right ventricle. The pacemaker output was set to 5&#x02009;V with a pulse width of 1.2&#x02009;ms and sensitivity set at 2.0&#x02009;mV. Several minutes after returning to the CCU from the catheterization laboratory, the patient developed ventricular fibrillation (VF) and lost consciousness; sinus rhythm was restored by external electrical defibrillation. ECG monitoring revealed that sensing failure occurred; pacemaker stimulations were delivered during the T wave, which triggered VF (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="anec70135-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>). The sensing issue was resolved by increasing the sensitivity to 0.5&#x02009;mV, and the bedside chest X&#x02010;ray showed that the position of the pacing catheter was satisfactory, so the pacing catheter was not repositioned. Following the second PCI, the patient experienced chest pain and discomfort. The ECG showed ST segment elevation in the inferior leads, and troponin levels increased again compared with the levels before the second PCI, suggesting the occurrence of another myocardial infarction (MI). The temporary pacemaker was removed 2&#x02009;days later, and the patient was discharged successfully 6&#x02009;days after the procedure.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="anec70135-fig-0001"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>ECG monitoring before and after ventricular fibrillation (VF) episodes. The ECG monitoring showed ST segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF (red asterisks) and ST segment depression in leads I and aVL (blue asterisks), which demonstrated ischemia in the right coronary artery (RCA) region, and the progressively earlier pacing spikes on T waves (blue arrows) as well as the R&#x02010;on&#x02010;T pacing causing VF (red arrows).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ANEC-31-e70135-g001" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-3"/></fig></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="anec70135-sec-0003"><label>3</label><title>Discussion</title><p>This case involves pacemaker sensing dysfunction (undersensing) caused by myocardial ischemia, which subsequently triggered malignant ventricular arrhythmias.</p><p>When a pacemaker is undersensing, it operates in a manner similar to asynchronous pacing. Asynchronous pacing itself does not directly cause malignant ventricular arrhythmias. Pacemakers initially used asynchronous pacing when they were first invented. Nowadays, asynchronous pacing is still used in certain special scenarios. For example, it is applied during the initial phase of pacemaker programming, during surgery involving an electrosurgical knife, and before MRI examinations (Russo et&#x000a0;al.&#x000a0;<xref rid="anec70135-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">2017</xref>). In these situations, the likelihood of malignant ventricular arrhythmias induced by asynchronous pacing is very low.</p><p>Malignant ventricular arrhythmias induced by asynchronous pacing usually occur in the presence of &#x0201c;vulnerable myocardium&#x0201d; when a pacemaker stimulus falls during the vulnerable period of the cardiac cycle. The most common cause of &#x0201c;vulnerable myocardium&#x0201d; is ischemia, typically resulting from MI (McLeod and Jokhi&#x000a0;<xref rid="anec70135-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">2004</xref>). In addition, it can also occur in severe electrolyte imbalances, significant disturbances in the internal environment, and other rare situations. For example, one case reported VF provoked by direct current cardioversion for atrial fibrillation combined with asynchronous pacing (Bohm et&#x000a0;al.&#x000a0;<xref rid="anec70135-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">2000</xref>).</p><p>The no&#x02010;reflow phenomenon is an acute reduction in coronary blood flow, which is characterized by the inadequate myocardial perfusion of a given segment without angiographic evidence of persistent mechanical obstruction of epicardial vessels. The mechanisms underlying the no&#x02010;reflow phenomenon include ischemia&#x02013;reperfusion injury and distal microthromboembolism (Kaur et&#x000a0;al.&#x000a0;<xref rid="anec70135-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">2021</xref>). As a result, it can lead to myocardial ischemia, which increases electrophysiological vulnerability.</p><p>The factors that contribute to &#x0201c;vulnerable myocardium&#x0201d; can also cause pacemaker sensing dysfunction simultaneously. When healthy myocardium is affected by ischemia (typically due to MI), local myocardial damage can lead to a reduction in local electrical potential, resulting in pacemaker undersensing. Our center previously reported a case of markedly decreased sensing parameters during a MI in a patient with a permanent pacemaker (Wu et&#x000a0;al.&#x000a0;<xref rid="anec70135-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">2022</xref>).</p><p>Therefore, caution should be exercised when a patient with myocardial ischemia, whether due to MI or acute reduction in coronary blood flow, presents with sensing dysfunction while using a temporary pacemaker. The situation can be addressed by increasing the pacemaker sensitivity and/or adjusting the position of the pacing catheter to achieve normal sensing parameters (typically, the right ventricular R wave should be &#x0003e;&#x02009;5&#x02009;mV).</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions" id="anec70135-sec-0004"><label>4</label><title>Conclusion</title><p>Acute reduction in coronary blood flow can lead to myocardial ischemia, thereby increasing the risk of pacemaker&#x02010;induced malignant ventricular arrhythmias. This case highlights the importance of careful monitoring and adjustment of pacemaker settings in patients with myocardial ischemia.</p></sec><sec id="anec70135-sec-0009"><title>Author Contributions</title><p>
<bold>Bo Wu:</bold> writing &#x02013; original draft, investigation, data curation. <bold>Yaqin Chen:</bold> writing &#x02013; original draft, formal analysis. <bold>Jianjun Tang:</bold> validation, supervision. <bold>Jia He:</bold> writing &#x02013; review and editing, conceptualization.</p></sec><sec id="anec70135-sec-0008"><title>Funding</title><p>Financial support was obtained from the Provincial Natural Science Foundation of Hunan grant No. 2022JJ40672.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="anec70135-sec-0005"><title>Conflicts of Interest</title><p>The authors declare no conflicts of interest.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supporting information</title><supplementary-material id="anec70135-supitem-0001" position="float" content-type="local-data"><caption><p>
